-- title:
--   Proof of Theorem 8, p. 207 — the iterates of Fig. 9 stay in the level set 𝓛 and f(x_k) strictly decreases
-- statement:
--   Let $f$ be twice continuously differentiable on an open set $D$ containing the box $\mathcal F=\{x: l\le x\le u\}$. Let $\{x_k\}$ be generated by the interior-reflective algorithm of Fig. 9 with parameters $0<\sigma_l<\sigma_u<1$ and $\rho>0$, descent directions $s_k$ and stepsizes $\alpha_k$. Then for every $k$
--   $$x_k\in\mathcal L=\{x\in\mathcal F: f(x)\le f(x_1)\}\qquad\text{and}\qquad f(x_{k+1})<f(x_k).$$
--
--   This is the first line of the proof of Theorem 8: by (3.4) each step decreases $f$ by more than $\sigma_l$ times a nonpositive quantity, so the iterates never leave the level set of the starting point. It is what makes the compactness assumption on $\mathcal L$ usable along the run.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The page writes the telescoped sum $f(x_m)-f(x_1)<\sum_{k<m}(\cdots)\le 0$; the statement is its per-step form together with membership in $\mathcal L$. The nonpositivity of $g_k^{T}s_k$ comes from $s_k$ being a descent direction at an interior point, and the smoothness hypothesis makes $g_k$ the true gradient. Compactness of $\mathcal L$ is not needed and is not assumed. `x 0` is the paper's $x_1$.
-- source:
--   Coleman & Li, On the convergence of interior-reflective Newton methods for nonlinear minimization subject to bounds, Math. Programming 67 (1994), p. 207, proof of Theorem 8 (first display)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LewisTorczon_BoundPS_Box
import Definitions.Def_ReflNewton_FirstOrder_Setting
open Filter Topology

namespace ReflNewton.FirstOrder

theorem iterates_mem_levelSet
    {n : ℕ} (l u : Fin n → EReal) (hl : ∀ i, l i ≠ ⊤) (hu : ∀ i, u i ≠ ⊥) (hlu : l ≤ u)
    (f : E n → ℝ) (D : Set (E n)) (hDo : IsOpen D)
    (hFD : LewisTorczon.BoundPS.box l u ⊆ D) (hf : ContDiffOn ℝ 2 f D)
    (σl σu ρ : ℝ) (hσl : 0 < σl) (hσlu : σl < σu) (hσu : σu < 1) (hρ : 0 < ρ)
    (x s : ℕ → E n) (α : ℕ → ℝ) (hrun : IsIRRun l u f σl σu ρ x s α) :
    ∀ k, x k ∈ LewisTorczon.BoundPS.levelSet l u f (x 0) ∧ f (x (k + 1)) < f (x k) := by sorry

end ReflNewton.FirstOrder
